# Documentation system overview

This repository builds documentation in two stages and merges them into a single site:

1) API reference site (`mkdocs-api-ref.yml`) — auto-generated API reference
2) Main documentation site (`mkdocs.yml`) — narrative docs and interactive Marimo content

The merged site mounts the API reference under `/reference/`. The main site includes a nav entry pointing there: `API Reference: /reference`.

---

## Stage 1: API reference site (generated, mkdocstrings-driven)

Config: `mkdocs-api-ref.yml`  
Purpose: A comprehensive, navigable API reference that stays in sync with the codebase.

Key traits:

- Pages and navigation are generated at build time by `docs_scripts/gen_ref_pages.py`.
- Supporting helpers in `docs_scripts/ref_introspect.py` and `docs_scripts/ref_render.py` separate:
  - API discovery/introspection, and
  - generated Markdown rendering.
- A post-processing hook `docs_scripts/api_hooks.py` cleans up mkdocstrings rendering artifacts, prunes redundant TOC entries, rewrites links/citations, and normalizes inherited-doc renders.
- This build also generates the shared API inventory used by both documentation stages.
- The API site is stable and predictable: module pages, class pages, consistent ordering, and no manual nav maintenance.

Why this stage exists:

- API reference generation benefits from automation and strict conventions.
- The API inventory must exist before the main docs build so `api:` links in the narrative docs can be resolved.
- Separating this stage from the Marimo site avoids mixing interactive tutorial requirements with API generation complexity.

---

## Stage 2: Main documentation site (Marimo-first)

Config: `mkdocs.yml`  
Purpose: User-facing docs, tutorials, and interactive content.

Key traits:

- Uses the `marimo` MkDocs plugin so tutorials can be:
  - run locally via `marimo edit`, and
  - published as interactive documentation.
- Navigation is primarily hand-curated (e.g., “Getting Started”, “Developer Zone”).
- This site is the primary entry point and links to the API site at `/reference/`.
- It consumes the generated API inventory so author-written `api:` links can be rewritten to `/reference/...`.

Why this stage exists:

- Marimo notebooks are a good format for executable tutorials and interactive docs.
- Keeping this site focused on narrative content avoids turning the docs into an API dump.

---

## Why the two-stage setup?

This split enables both:

1) A high-quality API reference (mkdocstrings) that is automatically generated, complete (including `__init__.py` content), and consistently structured.  
2) Interactive tutorials (Marimo) without compromising local edit/run workflows.

In practice: the main site is optimized for learning/onboarding; the API site is optimized for lookup/completeness.

---

## Differences from a standard mkdocstrings setup

A typical mkdocstrings workflow often:

- Manually writes Markdown pages under `docs/`
- Adds a few `::: package.module` directives
- Optionally enables `members: true` and lets mkdocstrings render everything
- Manually maintains MkDocs navigation

This repository differs in several ways:

### 1) Pages and navigation are generated automatically (API site)

- The `gen-files` plugin runs `docs_scripts/gen_ref_pages.py` during the API build.
- It writes module pages, class pages, a generated `SUMMARY.md` nav file, and the API inventory.
- The `literate-nav` plugin uses `SUMMARY.md` so the sidebar is always up to date.

### 2) `__init__.py` is treated as real API surface

Many projects expose public API via packages’ `__init__.py` files. This generator parses `__init__.py` like any other module (while still rendering it as the package landing page), ensuring those definitions are documented.

### 3) Members tables are custom (AST- and introspection-based), not mkdocstrings defaults

For module/class member variables and properties, the API reference uses custom collection logic rather than mkdocstrings’ default member rendering.

These tables can include:

- name
- type hint string
- value (or `unset`)
- a doc “hint” convention: a string literal immediately after the declaration
- inherited members marked as inherited
- property-specific handling (including read-only/abstract property labeling)

This is done because mkdocstrings does not reliably surface all variable-style docs in the same way, and this repository relies on conventions that are easier to capture from source and introspection.

### 4) Inheritance-aware documentation enhancements

The generator uses runtime introspection for class MRO and method classification to support:

- consistent method ordering
- inherited-only method stubs for members not declared on the derived class
- inherited member variables and properties
- fallback documentation ownership for undocumented overrides

For undocumented overrides with base-class docs, the system now renders the base method through mkdocstrings and then post-processes the HTML so:

- the derived method remains the canonical heading/signature/anchor on the page
- the inherited doc body is reused
- duplicate base-method TOC entries are removed

This keeps inherited documentation much closer to the normal mkdocstrings rendering path and makes link/citation/debug behavior more uniform.

### 5) Minimal but targeted HTML post-processing improves readability and consistency

A hook (`docs_scripts/api_hooks.py`) performs a few controlled HTML-space rewrites that are difficult to do reliably in Markdown space, including:

- removing repeated intro paragraphs in marked generated blocks
- pruning redundant class/base-class anchors from the right-hand TOC
- rewriting rendered `api:` links and selected type spans
- rewriting citations to point at the generated bibliography page
- stripping inherited base-render wrappers so only the inherited doc body remains where appropriate

The hook operates only on known generated patterns/markers to keep changes controlled.

